[rcb@netcom.uucp (Roy Bixler) writes:]
[] I was once again attempting to start up an interesting game and ran into
[] a peculiarly situated chest.  As a matter of course, I try to loot chests
[] whenever possible, but when I tried to move onto this chest, I ended up
[] falling through a trap door!  Which leads me to wonder: would it make more
[] sense if the chest fell through the trap door too and was perhaps smashed
[] at the bottom of its fall?

don't forget the 10 ft. square rule: each space on the screen represents
a 10 ft. square. so your chest was situated firmly on solid ground and
when you attempted to reach it you fell through an unavoidable trapdoor.
now one could argue that having discovered a trapdoor that one could
reasonably avoid falling thru if one were appropriately dexterous. you
could always still force a fall using the ">" command.

[] Here's my question from before which was never answered but still is a
[] curiosity for me.  What exactly does the dexterity statistic cover?

what exactly? i dunno ... how about approximately???

dexterity affects what you might expect, i.e. your ability to kick,
to throw, to manipulate weapons, to manipulate locks, to avoid certain
attacks and so on.

it may be preferable to max-out this stat (i.e. Dx:25) as opposed to
strength since there is an apparent limit to how strong you can be
above which your strength stat doesn't really matter! this doesn't
mean that gauntlets of power don't have a good use ...

